<section class="nc-guide hub-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The factors behind the conversions</h2>
    <p>Most everyday conversions rest on a handful of defined values. Knowing them makes it easy to sanity-check any result:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th class="num">Exact or standard value</th><th>Converter</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 inch</td><td class="num">2.54 cm (exact)</td><td><a href="../calculators/length.html">Length</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 mile</td><td class="num">1.609344 km (exact)</td><td><a href="../calculators/length.html">Length</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 pound</td><td class="num">0.45359237 kg (exact)</td><td><a href="../calculators/weight.html">Weight</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 U.S. gallon</td><td class="num">3.785411784 L (exact)</td><td><a href="../calculators/volume.html">Volume</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 acre</td><td class="num">43,560 sq ft</td><td><a href="../calculators/area.html">Area</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 square meter</td><td class="num">10.7639 sq ft</td><td><a href="../calculators/area.html">Area</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>°F from °C</td><td class="num">°C × 9/5 + 32</td><td><a href="../calculators/temperature.html">Temperature</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Where conversions matter in other calculators</h2>
    <p>Many NumberCove tools mix units, and a conversion error there costs real money:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Concrete</strong> is measured in feet and inches but ordered in cubic yards, where 1 cubic yard is 27 cubic feet. The <a href="../calculators/concrete.html">concrete calculator</a> converts each field separately.</li>
      <li><strong>Shipping</strong> bills by weight or by size: box dimensions in inches become a "dimensional weight" in pounds. See the <a href="../calculators/dimensional-weight.html">dimensional weight calculator</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Paint, flooring, and tile</strong> are priced per square foot or per gallon. A room measured in meters needs converting before you compare against coverage on the can or box.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Percentages are a conversion too</h2>
    <p>A percentage converts a part into a share of 100, which is why most percentage mistakes come from dividing by the wrong number. The <a href="../calculators/percentage.html">percentage calculator</a> handles the 5 common forms, from "what is 15% of 80" to percent change and reverse discounts. The <a href="everyday-math/">everyday math page</a> walks through the traps, such as why a 20% drop and a 20% rise don't cancel out.</p>
  </div>
</section>
